From: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fileio: do not size read() from malloc_usable_size()

read_virtual_file_fd() allocates buf = malloc(size + 1) and then grows
`size` to malloc_usable_size(buf) - 1 before calling read(fd, buf, size + 1).
With _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3 (the gcc default on Ubuntu 24.04) gcc derives a
*dynamic* object size for `buf` from the malloc() call, so __read_chk
receives buflen = size + 1 but nbytes = malloc_usable_size(buf), which can
be larger whenever glibc hands back a bigger chunk than requested ->
"*** buffer overflow detected ***" -> SIGABRT.  Reproduced with the
host-built `systemd --bus-introspect list` (called from the
export-dbus-interfaces meson target): get_oom_score_adjust() ->
read_virtual_file_fd() -> __read_chk -> __chk_fail.

The allocation above is already bounded by READ_VIRTUAL_BYTES_MAX + 1 and
min()ed against max_size, so `size` needs no adjustment; the
malloc_usable_size() opportunism only ever made the read request more
bytes than the allocation expression advertises.

Fixed differently upstream in v251; this is the minimal backport for 250.4.

Signed-off-by: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/basic/fileio.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/src/basic/fileio.c
+++ b/src/basic/fileio.c
@@ -470,8 +470,12 @@
                 if (!buf)
                         return -ENOMEM;

-                /* Use a bigger allocation if we got it anyway, but not more than the limit. */
-                size = MIN3(MALLOC_SIZEOF_SAFE(buf) - 1, max_size, READ_VIRTUAL_BYTES_MAX);
+                /* The allocation above is exactly size + 1 bytes.  Do not grow `size`
+                 * from MALLOC_SIZEOF_SAFE() here: with _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3 gcc derives
+                 * the dynamic object size of `buf` from the malloc() call above, and
+                 * __read_chk aborts at runtime when the read length exceeds it
+                 * (glibc may return a larger chunk than requested). */
+                size = MIN3(size, max_size, READ_VIRTUAL_BYTES_MAX);

                 for (;;) {
                         ssize_t k;
